1. what is diff bw OLTP and OLAP?
| Feature                | **OLTP (Online Transaction Processing)**           | **OLAP (Online Analytical Processing)**             |
| ---------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| **Purpose**            | Handle **real-time transactions**                  | Perform **complex analysis and reporting**          |
| **Main Users**         | Operational users (cashiers, app users, CRM staff) | Business analysts, data scientists, executives      |
| **Data Type**          | Current, transactional data                        | Historical, aggregated data                         |
| **Operations**         | INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, SELECT                     | SELECT with joins, aggregations, drill-downs        |
| **Query Type**         | Short, simple queries                              | Long, complex analytical queries                    |
| **Schema Design**      | **Normalized** to reduce redundancy                | **De-normalized** (Star/Snowflake schema) for speed |
| **Speed Optimization** | Optimized for **fast write operations**            | Optimized for **fast read operations**              |
| **Data Volume**        | Smaller (current state of business)                | Much larger (historical data over time)             |
| **Backup/Recovery**    | Frequent and essential                             | Periodic and planned                                |
| **Concurrency**        | Supports high concurrency (many users at once)     | Fewer users querying at the same time               |
| **Example Queries**    | “Insert new order”, “Update inventory”             | “What were total sales in Q1 by product category?”  |

2. Star Schema vs Snowflake Schema ?

| Feature                   | **Star Schema** 🌟                                             | **Snowflake Schema** ❄️                                      |
| ------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **Structure**             | Central fact table linked to **denormalized** dimension tables | Central fact table linked to **normalized** dimension tables |
| **Normalization**         | Dimension tables are **not normalized** (flattened)            | Dimension tables are **normalized** (split into sub-tables)  |
| **Joins**                 | Fewer joins needed (faster queries)                            | More joins needed (slower queries)                           |
| **Query Performance**     | Faster for read-heavy operations                               | Slightly slower due to more complex joins                    |
| **Storage Space**         | Consumes more storage due to repeated data                     | More efficient storage due to reduced redundancy             |
| **Design Complexity**     | Simple and easy to understand                                  | More complex to design and maintain                          |
| **ETL Effort**            | Easier ETL pipelines                                           | Requires more transformation logic                           |
| **Best Use Case**         | When **query speed** and simplicity are more important         | When **data consistency** and space optimization matter      |
| **BI Tool Compatibility** | Ideal for tools like Power BI, Tableau                         | Can be more complex for BI tools                             |

3. ✅ 1. Fact Table
📌 Definition:
A fact table stores measurable, quantitative data — typically numeric values that analysts want to aggregate (e.g., sales, revenue, quantity).
