8.🕰️ Temporal & Bi-Temporal Data Models in Data Warehousing
Temporal data models are designed to track changes to data over time. This is especially important for auditability, historical reporting, and compliance.

1. ⏳ Temporal Model
A temporal model tracks the valid time of data — i.e., when the data was true in the real world.
| customer\_id | customer\_name | city    | valid\_from | valid\_to  |
| ------------ | -------------- | ------- | ----------- | ---------- |
| 101          | Alice          | Toronto | 2020-01-01  | 2023-05-15 |
| 101          | Alice          | Ottawa  | 2023-05-16  | NULL       |
Meaning: Alice moved from Toronto to Ottawa on 2023-05-16.

2. 🧠 Bi-Temporal Model
A bi-temporal model tracks both:
✅ Valid Time — when the data is valid in real-world business terms
🛠️ Transaction Time — when the data was recorded in the system
This allows you to reconstruct past beliefs about the past, useful for legal, financial, and compliance use cases.

| customer\_id | city    | valid\_from | valid\_to  | transaction\_from | transaction\_to |
| ------------ | ------- | ----------- | ---------- | ----------------- | --------------- |
| 101          | Toronto | 2020-01-01  | 2023-05-15 | 2020-01-01        | 2023-05-20      |
| 101          | Ottawa  | 2023-05-16  | NULL       | 2023-05-20        | NULL            |
This means the system learned on 2023-05-20 that Alice moved to Ottawa on 2023-05-16.
