11. 1. ✅ Primary Key (PK)
Uniquely identifies each row in a table.
Mostly used in dimension tables.
📌 Example: In dim_customer, customer_sk (surrogate key) is the primary key.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
12.🔁 Foreign Key (FK)
Links a fact table to a dimension table.
Ensures referential integrity.
📌 Example: In fact_sales, customer_sk is a foreign key referencing dim_customer.

-------------------------------------------------
13. 🆔 Surrogate Key
A system-generated unique identifier (usually an auto-incrementing integer).
Used in dimension tables to uniquely identify rows and track historical changes (especially with SCDs).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
14. 🧾 Natural (Business) Key
Comes from the source system, represents a real-world entity.
Often used to detect changes during ETL.
📌 Example: customer_id, product_code
Natural keys are not reliable for uniquely identifying rows over time due to data changes, which is why surrogate keys are preferred in DWs.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
15.  🔄 Alternate Key
An alternative candidate key other than the primary key.
Often used for lookup operations or ensuring uniqueness on certain columns.
📌 Example: email might be an alternate key in dim_customer.
-----------------------------------------------------------------
16.🧠 Composite Key
A key made of two or more columns that together uniquely identify a row.
Common in factless fact tables or bridge tables.
📌 Example: In fact_attendance:
PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_id, date_id)

